new file mode 100644
index 0000000..05b75c0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/developer/adding-a-tool.md
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# Adding a tool
+
+A tool is a name, a toolset, a JSON schema the model sees and a handler. Built-in tools live under
+`src/indus/tools/builtin/`; plugins add tools the same way through their context.
+
+## The handler
+
+```python
+from collections.abc import Mapping
+from typing import Any
+
+WORD_COUNT_SCHEMA = {
+    "name": "word_count",
+    "description": "Count the words in a text file. Use it before summarising long files.",
+    "parameters": {
+        "type": "object",
+        "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "file to count"}},
+        "required": ["path"],
+    },
+}
+
+
+def word_count(args: Mapping[str, Any]) -> str:
+    with open(args["path"], encoding="utf-8") as handle:
+        return str(len(handle.read().split()))
+```
+
+The handler receives the arguments as a dictionary and returns text (or a multimodal result).
+Synchronous handlers run on a worker pool; `async def` handlers run on the event loop. Extra
+keyword parameters the handler declares, such as the session's scope, are filled in by the
+dispatcher. An exception becomes a tool error the model can read; secrets in it are masked.
+
+## Registering it
+
+In a built-in module, define `register_tools(registry)` and add the module to `BUILTIN_MODULES`
+in `src/indus/tools/builtin/__init__.py`:
+
+```python
+from indus.tools.registry import ToolRegistry, ToolTraits
+
+
+def register_tools(registry: ToolRegistry) -> None:
+    registry.register(
+        "word_count", "file", WORD_COUNT_SCHEMA, word_count, traits=ToolTraits(read_only=True)
+    )
+```
+
+In a plugin, call `ctx.register_tool(...)` from `register(ctx)` with the same arguments.
+
+Useful options:
+
+- `probe`: a function of the scope that says whether the tool can run here (a key is set, a
+  program is installed). Unavailable tools are left out of the session.
+- `requires_secrets`: the secret names the tool reads, shown in the reference and in `indus tools`.
+- `traits`: `read_only` tools may run in parallel with others; `path_arg` and `writer` let the
+  loop know which file a call touches.
+- `timeout_s`, `max_result_chars`: limits for one call.
+
+Add the tool to a toolset in `src/indus/tools/toolsets.py` (or register it into an existing one),
+then regenerate the reference pages: `python scripts/docs/gen_reference.py`. A test checks that
+every registered tool appears in [Tools and toolsets](../reference/tools.md).
+
+## Testing
+
+Call the handler directly for its logic, and run it through `indus.tools.dispatch` with a fake
+scope for the approval and policy paths. Never call real services in unit tests; the fakes under
+`tests/fakes/` cover models, web providers, backends and platforms.
diff --git a/docs/user/developer/architecture.md b/docs/user/developer/architecture.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..42d18dc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/developer/architecture.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Architecture
+
+Indus Agent is one Python package, `indus`, plus TypeScript front ends (the terminal UI, the desktop
+app and the web dashboard) that talk to it over a JSON-RPC protocol.
+
+## Layers
+
+The package is split into layers; a module may import only from layers below its own, and CI
+enforces it (`uv run lint-imports`). From the top:
+
+| Layer | Packages | What lives there |
+|---|---|---|
+| Surfaces | `indus.cli`, `indus.tui_bridge`, `indus.gateway`, `indus.web`, `indus.acp`, `indus.mcp_serve` | the ways in: command line, terminal UI bridge, messaging gateway and daemon, dashboard and headless backend, editor protocol, MCP server |
+| Research | `indus.research` | batch runs and trajectory tools |
+| Service | `indus.service` | sessions, the slash-command registry, the session host the surfaces share, RPC dispatch |
+| Learning and extensions | `indus.learning`, `indus.plugins`, `indus.mcp`, `indus.cron` | memory, skills, curator; plugins and hooks; the MCP client; scheduled jobs |
+| Core | `indus.core` | the agent loop: prompt building, model calls, tool rounds, compression, interrupts |
+| Tools | `indus.tools` | the tool registry, toolsets, dispatch and the built-in tools |
+| Backends | `indus.backends` | where commands run: local, Docker, SSH and cloud sandboxes |
+| Providers and safety | `indus.providers`, `indus.safety` | model providers and transports; approvals, redaction, URL and path policies |
+| Protocol and state | `indus.protocol`, `indus.state` | the wire models; the SQLite session store |
+| Foundation and platform | `indus.foundation`, `indus.platform` | config, home layout, scope, secrets, clock, logging; facts about the host |
+
+## A turn
+
+1. A surface submits the person's message to a session (`prompt.submit`).
+2. The service saves the message, then the agent loop builds the prompt (persona, rules files,
+   memory, skills index, tool schemas) and calls the model.
+3. Tool calls are checked (approvals, path and URL policies), run through the registry, and their
+   results saved; the loop calls the model again until it answers without tools or a limit is hit.
+4. Events stream to every viewer of the session as they happen: text and reasoning deltas, tool
+   start and end, status, the final message. Questions for the person (approvals, clarifications,
+   secrets) travel as server requests that any attached surface may answer; the first answer wins.
+
+## Profiles and scope
+
+One process can serve several profiles. Code never reads the home folder, environment, config or
+secrets from process globals: it reads them from the current `Scope` (`indus.foundation.scope`),
+which carries the profile through tasks and threads. Work started without a scope fails loudly
+instead of landing in the wrong profile.
+
+## Processes
+
+- `indus` alone hosts the agent in its own process (`--local`) or attaches to the profile's
+  **gateway daemon**, which owns the sessions, the messaging platforms and the scheduler.
+- The terminal UI is a TypeScript program that speaks the protocol to the daemon (or to a bridge
+  process it starts).
+- The desktop app finds or starts a backend and connects to it over a WebSocket with single-use
+  tickets; the dashboard is served by the same web backend.
+
+## Determinism in tests
+
+Time comes from an injected `Clock`, so tests never sleep; the fake model in `tests/fakes/model`
+replays scripted streams in every wire format the providers use.
diff --git a/docs/user/developer/protocol.md b/docs/user/developer/protocol.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..759f37a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/developer/protocol.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# The protocol
+
+Surfaces talk to the agent host with JSON-RPC 2.0 messages over stdio (the terminal UI bridge, the
+editor adapter) or a WebSocket (the daemon, the desktop app, the dashboard). The Python models in
+`indus.protocol` are the single source; `python -m indus.protocol.codegen` generates the
+TypeScript types and catalogue in `packages/protocol`, and CI fails when they are stale.
+
+Every method, event and error code is listed in the generated [Protocol](../reference/protocol.md)
+reference.
+
+## The shape of a session
+
+1. `session.create` or `session.resume` returns a session id.
+2. `prompt.submit` starts a turn. When the session is busy the request says what to do
+   (`when_busy`): queue it, steer the running turn, or interrupt and redirect.
+3. The host streams **events** for the session: `turn.started`, text and reasoning deltas, tool
+   started, progress and completed, status, and `turn.done` with how the turn ended.
+4. When the agent needs the person, the host sends a **server request** (approval, clarify, sudo,
+   secret). Any attached client may answer with `server_request.answer`; the first answer wins and
+   the others see it settled.
+5. `session.interrupt` stops a turn; `session.steer` adds guidance to it.
+
+## Clients and capabilities
+
+A client announces what it can do with `client.capabilities` (for example that it can answer
+server requests, and which surface it is). A client that cannot answer is never sent a question.
+
+## Errors
+
+Error codes have one meaning across every method, grouped (invalid request, not found, conflict,
+unavailable, ...) so a client can handle a group without knowing every code. Codes that never
+answer a method call (sent in a WebSocket close, for example) are listed separately.
+
+## Attaching to the daemon
+
+Clients attach to a profile's daemon over a WebSocket with a single-use ticket (`indus gateway
+grant` prints one for the terminal UI). Several clients can view one session at once; events carry
+a generation so a client that reconnects can replay what it missed.
diff --git a/docs/user/getting-started/install.md b/docs/user/getting-started/install.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b2dba1a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/getting-started/install.md
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+# Install
+
+There are four ways to install Indus Agent. They all end with the same `indus` command and the same
+home folder (`~/.indus`), so you can switch later without losing anything.
+
+| Channel | Good for | Updates with |
+|---|---|---|
+| Install script (Linux, macOS) or PowerShell script (Windows) | most people | `indus update` |
+| Docker image | servers, always-on messaging | pulling a new image |
+| Nix flake | NixOS and Nix users | `nix flake update` |
+| Desktop app | people who prefer a window | the app itself |
+
+Indus Agent is not published to PyPI: installing it with `pip install` from a package index is not
+supported.
+
+## Install script (Linux and macOS)
+
+```bash
+curl -fsSL https://github.com/indusagi/indus-agent/releases/latest/download/install.sh | bash
+```
+
+The script downloads a pinned copy of [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) (checked against a SHA-256
+digest), gets a managed Python, checks out the release into `~/.indus/indus-agent`, installs the
+Python packages exactly as locked, puts `indus` on your `PATH` and then offers to run
+[`indus setup`](quickstart.md). Every download is checked against a digest pinned in the release; a
+mismatch stops the install.
+
+Useful options (run `bash install.sh --help` for all of them):
+
+| Option | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `--channel stable\|canary\|main` | which line to follow; `stable` is the default |
+| `--dir PATH` | where the code goes (default `~/.indus/indus-agent`) |
+| `--indus-home PATH` | another home folder |
+| `--non-interactive` | ask nothing; skip the setup questions |
+| `--skip-browser` | leave out the browser tools (Chromium is large) |
+| `--include-desktop` | also set up the desktop app |
+
+To pass options through the pipe: `curl -fsSL <url> | bash -s -- --channel canary`.
+
+## PowerShell script (Windows)
+
+```powershell
+irm https://github.com/indusagi/indus-agent/releases/latest/download/install.ps1 | iex
+```
+
+The same steps as the shell script, with the same options in PowerShell spelling (`-Channel`,
+`-IndusHome`, `-NonInteractive`). On Windows the command-line agent is best run inside WSL2; see
+[Windows and WSL2](windows.md) for what works where.
+
+## Docker
+
+```bash
+docker pull ghcr.io/indusagi/indus-agent:stable
+```
+
+The image runs the messaging gateway and the dashboard; the [Docker](../user-guide/docker.md) page
+has the compose files and the steps for Linux and Windows.
+
+## Nix
+
+The repository is a flake. Its packages are `default`, `minimal`, `messaging`, `tui` and `web`:
+
+```bash
+nix run github:indusagi/indus-agent -- --version
+nix profile install github:indusagi/indus-agent
+```
+
+NixOS and Home Manager modules are included. Updates come from Nix (`nix flake update` and a
+rebuild); `indus update` refuses to touch a Nix install.
+
+## Desktop app
+
+The desktop app ships installers for macOS (DMG), Windows (NSIS) and Linux (AppImage). On first
+start it looks for Indus Agent on your computer and, when there is none, offers to install it with
+the same steps as the install script, showing each one as it runs. See [Desktop app](../user-guide/desktop.md).
+
+## What gets installed
+
+The standard install includes the extras most people need: web search, document reading,
+pseudo-terminals, the dashboard, editor support (ACP), every messaging platform that installs
+from wheels, and (unless `--skip-browser`) the browser tools. Other optional parts are extras you
+add later, for example `voice` (local speech to text), `research` (dataset tools), `modal`,
+`daytona` and `vercel` (cloud sandboxes) or `matrix-e2ee` (encrypted Matrix rooms, Linux only).
+
+To add an extra to an install made by a script, add its name to the install's saved list and
+update:
+
+```bash
+indus update --install-id                     # prints this install's id
+indus config set updates.installs.<id>.extras "[web, docs, pty, dashboard, acp, messaging, browser, voice]" --force
+indus update
+```
+
+## Check the install
+
+<!-- test -->
+```console
+$ indus --version
+Indus Agent ...
+...
+```
+
+`indus --version` also says how Indus Agent was installed and where. `indus doctor` checks the rest
+of the setup and points at anything that needs fixing.
