--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/user-guide/models.md
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# Models and keys
+
+Indus Agent talks to one main model at a time, through a provider. The
+[provider list](../reference/providers.md) shows the ones it knows without setup; any other
+OpenAI-compatible endpoint can be added.
+
+## Choosing
+
+- `indus model` lists providers and their models and saves your choice (`model.provider`,
+  `model.default`).
+- `/model <name>` in a chat switches for that conversation; `/model <name> --global` also saves it.
+- `-m MODEL --provider NAME` on the command line applies to one run.
+
+With no provider set, Indus Agent picks one whose key it finds.
+
+## Keys
+
+<!-- test -->
+```console
+$ indus auth list
+No credentials yet. Add one with `indus auth add PROVIDER`.
+```
+
+`indus auth add <provider>` stores a key (asked for, hidden) or signs in, for providers that offer a
+browser or device-code sign-in (`--type oauth`). Keys go to the operating system's keychain when
+there is one (`security.secret_store`: `auto`, `keychain` or `file`). A key in the environment or in
+the profile's `.env` file under the provider's usual name (for example `OPENAI_API_KEY`) works too;
+[Environment variables](../reference/environment-variables.md#model-provider-keys) lists the names.
+
+A provider can hold several credentials, a *pool*: `indus auth list` shows them, `indus auth
+priority` orders them, and `credential_pool_strategies.<provider>` decides how the next one is picked
+(`fill_first`, `round_robin`, `least_used`, `random`). A credential that keeps failing (an exhausted
+quota, a revoked key) is benched for a while; `indus auth reset` puts it back.
+
+## Local models
+
+Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM and llama.cpp servers on their usual ports are known without a key:
+
+```bash
+indus config set model.provider ollama
+indus config set model.default llama3.2
+```
+
+## Your own endpoint
+
+Name a custom OpenAI-compatible provider under `providers:` in `config.yaml`:
+
+```yaml
+providers:
+  lab:
+    base_url: https://llm.lab.example/v1
+    key_env: LAB_API_KEY        # the variable (or keychain entry) holding its key
+    models: [lab-large, lab-small]
+model:
+  provider: lab
+  default: lab-large
+```
+
+Other keys an entry may carry include `api_mode`, `context_length`, `aliases`, `display_name` and
+`request_timeout_seconds`.
+
+## When the model fails
+
+`fallback_providers` is a list of routes tried in order when the main one fails (an outage, a rate
+limit that will not reset soon, a refused key): `indus fallback add` adds one interactively, `indus
+fallback list` shows the chain.
+
+Smaller jobs (titling a conversation, compressing old turns, judging an approval in smart mode,
+looking at images) use the main model unless you give them their own under `auxiliary.<task>`, for
+example a cheaper model for titles:
+
+```bash
+indus config set auxiliary.title.model gpt-4o-mini
+```
+
+## Reasoning effort
+
+`/reasoning <level>` (none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max, ultra) asks the model for more
+or less thinking where the provider supports it; `/reasoning show` and `hide` control whether the
+reasoning is displayed.
diff --git a/docs/user/user-guide/plugins-and-mcp.md b/docs/user/user-guide/plugins-and-mcp.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5b9eefb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/user-guide/plugins-and-mcp.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Plugins and MCP
+
+Three ways to extend Indus Agent, from lightest to heaviest:
+
+| | What it adds | Runs |
+|---|---|---|
+| MCP servers | tools from any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol | as a separate process or a remote service |
+| Shell hooks | your scripts at moments of a turn (before a tool runs, after a reply, ...) | as commands |
+| Plugins | Python code that registers tools, hooks, providers, platforms or slash commands | inside Indus Agent |
+
+## MCP servers
+
+```bash
+indus mcp add docs --url https://mcp.example.com/mcp --auth oauth
+indus mcp add fs --preset filesystem
+indus mcp add tickets --command npx --arg -y --arg @example/tickets-mcp --env TICKETS_TOKEN='${TICKETS_TOKEN}'
+indus mcp test fs
+```
+
+<!-- test -->
+```console
+$ indus mcp list
+No MCP servers are configured.
+Add one with `indus mcp add NAME --command CMD` or `indus mcp add NAME --url URL`.
+```
+
+Servers are saved under `mcp_servers` in `config.yaml`; secrets are written there only as `${NAME}`
+references to stored secrets. Each server's tools appear as the toolset `mcp-<name>`, so
+`indus tools` turns them on or off per platform like any other toolset. `/mcp` shows the connected
+servers in a chat and `/reload-mcp` reconnects after a change (the config file is also watched).
+
+A server can be `--lazy` (started on first use) and gets a trust tier: `untrusted` servers'
+tool results are marked as untrusted content for the model. Packages started through `npx`, `uvx`,
+`pipx`, `bunx` or `pnpm dlx` are checked against the OSV malware advisories before they start.
+
+Indus Agent is also an MCP server for other agents: see [Serving MCP](../mcp-serve.md).
+
+## Shell hooks
+
+```yaml
+hooks:
+  pre_tool_call:
+    - command: ~/bin/check-command.sh
+      matcher: terminal
+      timeout: 10
+```
+
+The hook gets the event as JSON on stdin and can answer on stdout, for example to block a tool call
+with a reason. Every hook command is shown to you and approved once before it first runs
+(`indus hooks list`, `indus hooks test`, `indus hooks revoke`); `--accept-hooks` or
+`hooks_auto_accept: true` skips the question.
+
+## Plugins
+
+```bash
+indus plugins install example/indus-weather --ref <40-character commit>
+indus plugins enable weather
+indus plugins list
+```
+
+A plugin is a folder with a manifest (`plugin.yaml` or `plugin.json`) and a Python module whose
+`register(ctx)` adds what the plugin offers. Installed plugins are listed but load only once enabled (`plugins.enabled`). Before an
+install the plugin is scanned: a dangerous finding refuses it, a cautionary one needs `--force`.
+`indus plugins capabilities` shows what a plugin asks to be allowed, and `plugins.entries.<name>.granted_capabilities` records what you allowed.
+
+Plugins inside a project (`.indus/plugins/`) load only with `plugins.project: true` and never in
+the messaging gateway. Updates are checked daily (`plugins.auto_update_check_hours`) and applied
+only when you say so (`indus plugins update`, or `plugins.auto_apply`).
+
+The [developer guide](../developer/adding-a-tool.md) shows how to write one.
diff --git a/docs/user/user-guide/profiles.md b/docs/user/user-guide/profiles.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0fa7a88
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/user-guide/profiles.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# Profiles
+
+A profile is a complete, separate Indus Agent: its own settings, keys, conversations, memories,
+skills, scheduled jobs and messaging gateway. Use one for work and one for personal projects, or
+give a team bot its own. The first profile is called `default` and lives in the home folder itself
+(`~/.indus`); others live under `~/.indus/profiles/<name>`.
+
+<!-- test -->
+```console
+$ indus profile create work --no-alias
+Created profile 'work' at ...
+Copied ...
+Use it with `indus -p work` or `indus profile use work`.
+```
+
+## Using a profile
+
+| How | Effect |
+|---|---|
+| `indus -p work ...` | this one command runs as `work` (the flag works in any position) |
+| `INDUS_PROFILE=work` | the same, from the environment |
+| `indus profile use work` | `work` becomes the profile used when nothing says otherwise |
+| an alias | `indus profile create` writes a small `work` command that starts `indus -p work`; `--no-alias` skips it |
+
+`indus profile list` shows them all; the sticky profile is marked. `/whoami` in a chat says which
+profile and home you are on.
+
+## Creating from another profile
+
+| Option | Copies |
+|---|---|
+| (none) | nothing: an empty profile with the bundled skills |
+| `--clone` | settings, `.env`, persona, memories and skills |
+| `--clone-all` | everything except history and runtime state |
+| `--clone-channels` | also the messaging channel settings and tokens |
+| `--clone-from NAME` | clone that profile instead of the current one |
+
+`indus profile describe work --text "Client projects"` records what a profile is for, and
+`indus profile show work` prints it with the profile's details. `rename` and `delete` do what they say (a deleted profile cannot be brought
+back, so take an `indus backup` first if in doubt).
+
+## What is shared
+
+Nothing by default. Each profile's gateway is a separate process with its own platform tokens: one
+bot token can serve only one profile at a time, and a second gateway that tries to use the same
+token is refused. The desktop app and the dashboard show every profile of the machine and switch
+between them.
diff --git a/docs/user/user-guide/research.md b/docs/user/user-guide/research.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9a90924
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/user-guide/research.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Research tooling
+
+`indus research` runs the agent over many prompts and turns the conversations into training and
+evaluation data. It is meant for people who study or fine-tune agents; everyday use never needs
+it. The dataset commands need the `research` extra.
+
+| Command | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `indus research batch DATASET` | run every prompt of a JSONL file through the agent and save each conversation as a trajectory |
+| `indus research distributions` | list the toolset distributions a batch can sample from |
+| `indus research swe` | run coding tasks with only a terminal tool and save their trajectories |
+| `indus research compress INPUT` | shorten trajectories to fit a token budget |
+| `indus research sample` | draw long trajectories from datasets, compress them and merge them into one file |
+
+## A batch run
+
+```bash
+indus research batch prompts.jsonl --run-name first --workers 4 --distribution default
+```
+
+Each line of `prompts.jsonl` holds a `"prompt"`. Every prompt runs in its own isolated session with
+a toolset drawn from the distribution (so the data covers different tool mixes), and the result is
+written to the run's folder as one trajectory per line. Runs resume: start the same command again
+and finished prompts are skipped. Progress, failures and token counts are kept per run, and a
+statistics file sums up how often each tool was used and how often it failed.
+
+The trajectory files follow the [trajectory format](../reference/trajectory-format.md). A normal
+chat can write the same samples as you work: `indus chat --save-trajectories` (or
+`research.save_trajectories: true`) appends each finished turn's conversation to
+`trajectory_samples.jsonl` in the working folder.
+
+## Compressing
+
+`indus research compress` keeps the start and the last turns of a long trajectory and replaces as
+few middle turns as needed with one summary turn, never separating a tool call from its result,
+until it fits `--target-max-tokens`, counted with a pinned tokenizer
+(`--tokenizer`, `--revision`). Remote tokenizer code never runs unless you pass
+`--trust-remote-code`. `--dry-run` shows what would happen.
+
+## Experiments
+
+To compare two versions of the agent on the same tasks, see [Evals](../research/evals.md).
